    "slug": "constant-acceleration-equations",
    "title": "Constant-acceleration equations explained",
    "category": "mechanics",
    "description": "Master the five SUVAT equations, learn which one to choose when time, displacement or final velocity is missing, and work through two fully solved examples.",
    "content": "# Constant-acceleration equations explained\n\n## Overview\n\nThe constant-acceleration equations, usually called the SUVAT equations, are the workhorse of GCSE and A-Level mechanics. They describe motion in a straight line when the acceleration is constant, which covers a surprising number of real situations: a car accelerating steadily away from traffic lights, a ball falling under gravity when air resistance is negligible, and a train braking at a steady rate.\n\nThe five symbols are s for displacement in metres, u for initial velocity in m/s, v for final velocity in m/s, a for acceleration in m/s² and t for time in seconds. The name SUVAT is just those five letters in order.\n\nSign convention matters more here than anywhere else in kinematics. Choose a positive direction before you start, and give every velocity, displacement and acceleration the sign that matches it. If an object is slowing down while moving in the positive direction, its acceleration is negative.\n\nThese equations assume motion in a straight line with constant acceleration. In projectile motion you apply them separately to the horizontal and vertical directions, because the horizontal acceleration is zero while the vertical acceleration is −g. In free fall, you can often take u = 0, which simplifies the equations considerably.\n\n## Key equation\n\nThe five SUVAT equations are:\n\n`v = u + at`\n\n`s = ut + ½at²`\n\n`s = vt − ½at²`\n\n`v² = u² + 2as`\n\n`s = (u + v)t / 2`\n\nThe first equation says the final velocity is the initial velocity plus the velocity gained through acceleration. The second gives displacement from the initial velocity and the extra distance covered as the object speeds up. The third is the same idea seen from the final velocity. The fourth is the only equation without time in it, so it is the one to use when the time is not given and not asked for.\n\n## Worked example\n\nAn object starts from rest and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 5 s.\n\nWrite down what you know: u = 0, a = 2 m/s² and t = 5 s. For the final velocity, use v = u + at, which leaves out s: v = 0 + 2 × 5 = 10 m/s. For the displacement, use s = ut + ½at², which leaves out v: s = 0 × 5 + ½ × 2 × 25 = 0 + 25 = 25 m. Note that ½ × 2 × 25 = 25, not 50, because the half and the two cancel. You can check the displacement with the average-velocity equation: the average velocity is (0 + 10) / 2 = 5 m/s, and in 5 s the displacement is 5 × 5 = 25 m, which agrees.\n\nNow consider a second example where time is not involved. A car travelling at 12 m/s brakes with a constant deceleration of 4 m/s² until it stops.\n\nWrite down what you know: u = 12 m/s, v = 0 and a = −4 m/s². You want s, and time is neither given nor asked for, so use v² = u² + 2as, the equation that leaves out t. Substituting gives 0² = 12² + 2 × (−4) × s, which is 0 = 144 − 8s. Rearranging gives 8s = 144, so s = 144 / 8 = 18 m. Written in rearranged form, s = (0 − 144) / (2 × −4) = (−144) / (−8) = 18 m. The stopping distance is 18 m, a sensible value for a car braking firmly from 12 m/s, which is about 43 km/h.\n\n## Common mistakes to avoid\n\n- Using a SUVAT equation when the acceleration is not constant. If a force changes during the motion, or air resistance grows with speed, these equations do not apply and you need another method.\n- Getting the sign of the acceleration wrong. A deceleration is a negative acceleration when the motion is in the positive direction. Entering +4 m/s² for braking in the example above would give a negative stopping distance, which is a clear warning.\n- Choosing an equation that contains a quantity you do not know. If time is not given and not asked for, use v² = u² + 2as. If final velocity is not involved, use s = ut + ½at². Listing knowns and unknowns before choosing saves more time than it costs.\n- Confusing s with distance travelled. The SUVAT displacement s is measured from the starting point in the chosen positive direction. If the object turns around, the displacement and the distance travelled are different.\n- Substituting speeds in km/h without converting. Divide km/h by 3.6 to get m/s before doing anything else.\n- Squaring incorrectly in v² = u² + 2as. The equation involves v² and u² separately: (12 m/s)² is 144 m²/s², and you must square before adding or subtracting. A common error is to write v = u + 2as, which mixes a velocity with an acceleration times a distance and fails a units check.\n\n## How to check your answer\n\nFirst, check the units. Every term in v = u + at has units of m/s, and every term in s = ut + ½at² has units of metres, since at² has units of (m/s²) × s² = m. If your units do not match, the equation has been misremembered or misapplied.\n\nSecond, check the answer against a second equation, as in the worked example, where the average-velocity route confirmed the 25 m displacement. Third, check the magnitude: an object accelerating at 2 m/s² for 5 s reaches 10 m/s, a fast sprint, and covers 25 m, roughly the length of a swimming pool.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Which SUVAT equation should I use?\n\nList the quantities you know and the one you want, then choose the equation that contains exactly those and leaves out the one you neither know nor need. If time is missing, use v² = u² + 2as. If final velocity is missing, use s = ut + ½at².\n\n### What does it mean if I get a negative displacement?\n\nIt means the final position is in the negative direction from the starting point, according to the positive direction you chose. That is a valid physical answer, not necessarily an error. But if the question describes an object moving forwards and stopping, a negative displacement means a sign has gone wrong somewhere, usually the acceleration.\n\n### Can I use SUVAT equations for a falling object?\n\nYes, provided air resistance is negligible so the acceleration is constant at g = 9.81 m/s². Take the direction of the fall as positive to keep the signs simple: then u is usually zero, a is +9.81 m/s² and s is the distance fallen. If you take upwards as positive instead, a is −9.81 m/s² and a falling object has negative displacement and negative velocity.\n\n\n### What if I know only two SUVAT quantities?\n\nYou cannot solve it yet — each SUVAT equation links four of the five quantities, so you need any three known values to find the other two. Re-read the question: the third value is often hidden, such as starting from rest (u = 0) or coming to a stop (v = 0).\n\n### Why do the SUVAT equations fail when acceleration changes?\n\nThey are derived by assuming the acceleration is constant. If the acceleration changes — for example a car whose engine force changes, or a falling object with growing air resistance — you must split the motion into parts or use a different method, such as a graph or numerical steps."
