    "description": "Use v = fλ to link wave speed, frequency and wavelength, from a 256 Hz tuning fork to the wavelength of visible light, with worked examples and checks.",
    "content": "# Wave properties: frequency, wavelength and speed\n\n## Overview\n\nWaves carry energy from one place to another without carrying matter with them. A water wave moves across a pond, but the water itself mostly moves up and down. A sound wave travels across a room, but the air molecules only vibrate back and forth around their starting positions.\n\nThe key quantities are frequency, wavelength, period, amplitude and speed. Frequency, f, is the number of waves passing a point each second, measured in hertz (Hz), where 1 Hz is one wave per second. Wavelength, λ, the Greek letter lambda, is the distance between two identical points on consecutive waves, such as crest to crest, measured in metres.\n\nThese quantities are linked by the wave equation, v = fλ. It says that the speed of a wave equals its frequency multiplied by its wavelength. If the frequency doubles while the speed stays the same, the wavelength halves, because more waves must fit into the same distance travelled each second.\n\nWaves come in two kinds. In a transverse wave, the vibration is at right angles to the direction the wave travels: light waves and waves on a string are transverse. In a longitudinal wave, the vibration is along the direction of travel: sound waves are longitudinal, with compressions and rarefactions travelling through the air.\n\n## Key equation\n\nThe wave equation is:\n\n`v = fλ`\n\nwhere v is the wave speed in m/s, f is the frequency in Hz and λ is the wavelength in metres. It rearranges to f = v / λ and to λ = v / f, so you can find any one quantity from the other two.\n\n`T = 1 / f` and `f = 1 / T`\n\nFor electromagnetic waves in a vacuum, the speed is the speed of light, c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s, so the equation becomes c = fλ. Because c is so large, visible light has a very high frequency, around 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz, and a very short wavelength, around 6 × 10⁻⁷ m. For sound in air, the speed is about 340 m/s at room temperature, so a sound of frequency 440 Hz, the note A above middle C, has a wavelength of just under a metre.\n\n## Worked example\n\nFirst, a tuning fork vibrates at a frequency of 256 Hz and produces a sound wave with a wavelength of 1.32 m.\n\nUse v = fλ: v = 256 × 1.32 = 337.92 m/s, which is about 338 m/s. That is close to the standard value of 340 m/s for sound in air, and the small difference is because the speed of sound depends on the temperature of the air. Check the units: hertz multiplied by metres gives (1/s) × m, which is m/s, as a speed should be.\n\nSecond, find the wavelength of a 440 Hz sound wave in air where the speed of sound is 340 m/s.\n\nRearrange the wave equation to λ = v / f: λ = 340 / 440 = 0.773 m. The wavelength is about 0.77 m, a little less than a metre. A higher-pitched sound has a shorter wavelength: a note at 880 Hz, one octave higher, would have a wavelength of 340 / 880 = 0.386 m, exactly half, because the frequency has doubled while the speed stayed the same.\n\nThird, find the wavelength of light with a frequency of 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Light travels at c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s in a vacuum.\n\nUse λ = c / f: λ = 3 × 10⁸ / 5 × 10¹⁴ = 6 × 10⁻⁷ m. That is 0.0000006 m, which is more usefully written as 600 nm, since 1 nm is 10⁻⁹ m and 6 × 10⁻⁷ m is 600 × 10⁻⁹ m. A wavelength of 600 nm is in the orange part of the visible spectrum.\n\n## Common mistakes to avoid\n\n- Confusing wavelength with amplitude. Wavelength is the horizontal distance between two crests, measured along the direction of travel. Amplitude is the vertical height from the rest position to a crest. They are different quantities with different units, and changing one does not change the other.\n- Forgetting that frequency is set by the source and does not change when a wave enters a new medium. When sound passes from air into water, its speed increases and its wavelength increases, but its frequency stays the same. If you change the frequency in the calculation, the answer will be wrong.\n- Using kilohertz or megahertz without converting. A frequency of 256 Hz is 256, but a frequency of 2 kHz is 2,000 Hz and a frequency of 100 MHz is 100,000,000 Hz. Check the prefix before substituting.\n\n## How to check your answer\n\nCheck the units first: frequency in hertz multiplied by wavelength in metres gives m/s, which is a speed. If your units do not work out, you have rearranged the equation incorrectly. If any check fails, re-check the numbers you substituted before trusting the result.\n\nNext, check the magnitude: sound wavelengths in air are typically between a few centimetres and a few metres, because audible frequencies run from about 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz and the speed is about 340 m/s. A calculated wavelength of 0.773 m for a 440 Hz note fits that range, while an answer of 773 m or 0.000773 m would not.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What is the difference between frequency and period?\n\nFrequency is how many waves pass a point each second, measured in hertz. Period is how long one wave takes to pass, measured in seconds. They are reciprocals: f = 1 / T and T = 1 / f.\n\n### Why does the frequency stay the same when a wave changes medium?\n\nBecause the frequency is determined by the source, not by the medium. Each vibration of the source produces one wave, and the waves cannot pile up or disappear at the boundary between two media, so the number of waves arriving at the boundary each second must equal the number leaving it each second.\n\n### Does a wave with a larger amplitude travel faster?\n\nNo. The speed of a wave depends on the medium, not on the amplitude. A larger amplitude means the wave carries more energy, and for sound it means a louder sound, but it does not travel any faster.\n\n\n### How are wavelength and frequency related for light?\n\nLight travels at c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s in a vacuum, so λ = c / f. A higher frequency means a shorter wavelength: red light (about 4.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz) has a wavelength near 700 nm, while blue light (about 6.7 × 10¹⁴ Hz) is near 450 nm.\n\n### What happens to the wavelength when a wave enters a slower medium?\n\nThe frequency stays the same — it is set by the source — so the wavelength must shrink, since v = fλ and v has fallen. This is why light bends when it enters glass: its speed and wavelength both drop while its frequency does not change."
