    "slug": "electric-power-energy",
    "title": "Electrical power and energy",
    "category": "electricity",
    "description": "Electrical power and energy explained with P = VI = I²R = V²/R, worked examples for a 60 W bulb and a 2 kW heater, and kilowatt-hours on your bill in full.",
    "content": "# Electrical power and energy\n\n## Overview\n\nElectrical power is the rate at which electrical energy is transferred, measured in watts, and it is the number that decides how bright a bulb shines, how quickly a kettle boils and how much a device costs to run. A 60 W bulb transfers 60 joules of electrical energy into light and heat every second, while a 2,000 W kettle transfers 2,000 joules per second and boils water far faster.\n\nPower is linked to voltage and current by P = VI: the power is the voltage multiplied by the current. That single equation, combined with Ohm’s law V = IR, gives two more forms, P = I²R and P = V²/R, which let you find the power from whichever quantities you know. Energy is then power multiplied by time, E = Pt, and it is the energy that you pay for, measured in kilowatt-hours on your electricity bill.\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Power is the rate of energy transfer: P = VI, measured in watts (1 W = 1 J/s).\n- With Ohm’s law, P = I²R = V²/R, so power grows with the square of current or voltage.\n- Energy is E = Pt, measured in joules, or in kilowatt-hours (1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J) on a bill.\n- A resistor’s power rating is the maximum it can dissipate safely; exceeding it causes overheating.\n- High-voltage transmission keeps current low, which minimises I²R losses in the cables.\n\n## Power from voltage and current\n\nThe defining equation is P = VI, where P is the power in watts, V is the potential difference in volts and I is the current in amps. It comes directly from the definitions: voltage is energy per coulomb, current is coulombs per second, and multiplying them gives joules per second, which is watts. A 12 V battery supplying 2 A delivers 24 W, and a 230 V mains supply delivering 0.26 A delivers about 60 W.\n\nUsing V = IR, the power can be rewritten in two useful ways. Substituting V = IR into P = VI gives P = I²R, which is the power dissipated in a resistor carrying current I. Substituting I = V / R gives P = V²/R, which is the power in a resistor with voltage V across it. All three forms give the same answer; choose the one that uses the quantities you know.\n\nThe I²R form is the one that matters for heating: the power turned into heat in a resistor depends on the square of the current. That is why a fuse is a thin wire that melts when the current gets too large — the I²R heating grows rapidly — and why a cable rated for 13 A must never carry 26 A, which would produce four times the heating.\n\n## Key equation\n\nThe power equations are:\n\n`P = VI = I²R = V²/R`\n\nwhere P is the power in watts (W), V is the potential difference in volts (V), I is the current in amps (A) and R is the resistance in ohms (Ω). Energy is:\n\n`E = Pt`\n\nwhere E is the energy in joules (J) and t is the time in seconds (s). In billing units, `1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J`, and the energy in kilowatt-hours is the power in kilowatts multiplied by the time in hours.\n\n## Worked example\n\nA light bulb is rated at 60 W when connected to a 230 V mains supply. Find the current it draws and its resistance. Then find the energy used by a 2 kW heater running for 3 hours, in joules and in kilowatt-hours.\n\nFirst, the current from P = VI: rearranging gives I = P / V = 60 / 230 = 0.2609 A, about 0.26 A. The resistance follows from P = V²/R, rearranged as R = V² / P = 230² / 60 = 52,900 / 60 = 881.7 Ω, about 882 Ω. As a check, use P = I²R with these values: I²R = 0.2609² × 881.7 = 0.06807 × 881.7 = 60.0 W, which matches the rating exactly.\n\nNext, the heater: its energy in kilowatt-hours is simply power times time, E = 2 kW × 3 h = 6 kWh. In joules, that is 6 × 3.6 × 10⁶ = 2.16 × 10⁷ J, or 21.6 MJ. To put that in perspective, it is the same energy as the 60 W bulb would use in 100 hours: 0.06 kW × 100 h = 6 kWh. The heater delivers the energy quickly and the bulb slowly, but the total is identical, and so is the cost.\n## Common mistakes to avoid\n\n- Confusing power and energy. Power is the rate, in watts; energy is the total, in joules or kilowatt-hours. A 2 kW heater has a high power, but if it runs for only a minute it uses little energy: 2,000 × 60 = 120,000 J, or 0.033 kWh.\n- Using the wrong power formula. P = V²/R needs the voltage across that particular resistor, not the battery voltage, and P = I²R needs the current through that resistor. In a series circuit the voltages differ, and in a parallel circuit the currents differ.\n- Forgetting to convert hours to seconds when working in joules. Energy in joules needs time in seconds; energy in kilowatt-hours needs time in hours. Mixing them gives an answer wrong by a factor of 3,600.\n\n## How to check your answer\n\nCheck the units first: volts times amps gives (J/C) × (C/s) = J/s = W, as power should be. Then check with a second form of the power equation: if you found the current from P = VI, confirm the power with P = I²R, as in the worked example where both routes give 60 W.\n\nTest a limiting case: doubling the voltage across a fixed resistor must quadruple the power, because P = V²/R, and doubling the current must quadruple it too. Finally, check the energy: a 2 kW heater for 3 hours must use 6 kWh, and converting to joules must give 6 × 3.6 × 10⁶ = 2.16 × 10⁷ J.\n\nUse the [electric power calculator](/calculators/electric-power) to check P = VI for any voltage and current, the [Ohm’s law calculator](/calculators/ohms-law) for the current or resistance, and the [resistance calculator](/calculators/resistance) when you know the power and voltage. The [series and parallel circuits guide](/guides/series-parallel-circuits) shows how power divides between resistors.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What is the difference between a watt and a kilowatt-hour?\n\nA watt is a rate of energy transfer — one joule per second — and it describes how powerful a device is right now. A kilowatt-hour is an amount of energy — the energy used by a 1 kW device in one hour — and it describes how much energy has been used in total. You pay for kilowatt-hours, not watts.\n\n### Why do electricity bills use kilowatt-hours instead of joules?\n\nBecause the numbers are more convenient. A typical household uses about 10 kWh per day, which is 36,000,000 J — a number nobody wants to read on a bill. The kilowatt-hour is simply a joule scaled up by 3.6 million to give everyday-sized numbers.\n\n### Why does a 100 W bulb shine brighter than a 60 W bulb?\n\nBecause it converts electrical energy into light and heat at a higher rate — 100 joules per second against 60. On the same 230 V supply, the 100 W bulb draws more current, 0.43 A against 0.26 A, and has a lower resistance, 529 Ω against 882 Ω, which is what allows the extra current to flow.\n\n### Why are power lines run at very high voltages?\n\nBecause the power lost as heat in the cables is I²R, and for a given power P = VI, a higher voltage means a lower current. Raising the voltage from 230 V to 400,000 V cuts the current by a factor of about 1,700 and the I²R losses by a factor of about three million, which is why the national grid uses such high voltages.\n\n### What does the power rating on a resistor mean?\n\nIt is the maximum power the resistor can dissipate as heat without overheating and failing. A 0.25 W resistor can safely handle up to 0.25 W; if your circuit asks it to dissipate more, you need a resistor with a higher rating, even if the resistance value is the same.\n\n### Is a kilowatt-hour the same as a unit of electricity?\n\nYes. Electricity suppliers call one kilowatt-hour one unit. A bill for 300 units means 300 kWh of energy were used, and the charge is 300 times the price per unit. A 2 kW heater running for 3 hours uses 6 units.\n"
